@GeoT326 Tripolitsa was indeed a genocide, I’d advise you to read what happened to those people. And yes it indeed was a genocide, it’s not about numbers, it is about the intent. Also “they didn’t belong there” isn’t an argument and the same is said about us by the Greek nationalists even today. “They didn’t leave and we killed them but it’s okay because they aren’t from here.” That’s a racist mindset and you won’t have coexistence with that; you should be ashamed of yourself to say that.

As I said, UNCLOS is irrelevant to this topic but if you wonder, no we won’t implement a convention that we didn’t sign. Greece will come to terms with us and we’ll make an agreement. And if Greece rejects it and tries pushing further, then at some point war will be inevitable and Greece will lose. With a non-existent army and failing demographics, Greece cannot defend itself.

You see, as long as you don’t take responsibility about your own history and compromise, you will always lose.

@Dr-Ekmek I don’t disagree with you on the reasoning as political. But the historical facts do exist. No matter what everyone says.
As far as Tripolitsa atrocities goes. Far from a Genocite definition. And don’t forget that those people didn’t belong there. They were warned to leave and they didn’t. But equalizing atrocities with Genocites is too much.
As far as the UNCLOS, it’s about the future. Civilized countries respect each other’s rights. If you believe that a good number of Greek islands and half of the Aegean Sea is Turkish, then our future is not promising.
